# November 22, 2026: Arizona beyond Cochise and Yavapai counties (region az). A first scatter across the rest of the
# state, one or two places per county: Yuma (the territorial prison, the quartermaster depot), La Paz (Swansea), Mohave
# (Oatman's Durlin Hotel, Pipe Spring's Winsor Castle), Coconino (Riordan Mansion, Wupatki), Apache (Hubbell Trading
# Post), Navajo (La Posada), Gila (the Globe courthouse), Pinal (Casa Grande Ruins, the 1891 Florence courthouse),
# Maricopa (Vulture City), Pima (Fort Lowell) and Santa Cruz (Tumacacori, Tubac).
# Facts come from the linked sources only: Wikipedia articles read as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py) and the managing
# bodies' pages (Arizona State Parks, NPS, BLM, the Yuma prison museum, the Tucson Presidio Trust, La Posada) read with
# tools/page.py. Pins were looked at on Esri satellite and LiDAR shading; each pin note says what that showed.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'az_bumv','label':'Arizona: a first scatter across the state','added':'2026-11-22 23:27'}
PLACES=[
N('yuma-territorial-prison','Yuma Territorial Prison','Yuma','az','inst','standing',32.72694,-114.615,'site','open','drive','','1876–1909',
  'The Arizona Territory\'s prison on the hill above the Colorado River crossing, which took its first inmate on July 1, 1876. In 33 years 3,069 prisoners, 29 of them women, served sentences here, and the prisoners themselves did the building and repairs. The last of them left in 1909 for the new prison at Florence. Yuma Union High School used the cellblocks as classrooms for four years from 1910. It is now a state historic park, one of the Yuma Crossing sites on the National Register.',
  'The cell block and its cells, the sally port the prisoners came in by, the Dark Cell used for punishment, and the guard tower, which visitors can climb; a museum tells the prison\'s story. The prison graveyard, where 104 prisoners are buried, is on the park grounds.',
  'Walk into the cells of the Old West\'s most famous prison.',
  'Yuma Territorial Prison State Historic Park, an Arizona state park run as a museum, at 220 Prison Hill Road. Parking at the main entrance, with an RV lot to the south and overflow below Cemetery Hill. Admission from the museum\'s page, read November 2026: adults $10, ages 7 to 13 $7, 6 and under free. No outside food.',
  [W('Yuma Territorial Prison'),['Yuma Territorial Prison: admission and hours','https://yumaprison.org/admission/'],['Yuma Territorial Prison: exhibits','https://yumaprison.org/exhibits/']],
  th=[32.72691,-114.61472],thn='Prison main entrance lot, Prison Hill Road',conf=None,brick='?',hours='October to May daily 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; June to September daily 8:30 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('yuma-quartermaster-depot','Yuma Quartermaster Depot (Colorado River State Historic Park)','Yuma','az','fort','standing',32.72711,-114.62335,'site','open','drive','','1864–1891',
  'The army supply depot set up in 1864 to store and send out supplies to the frontier posts of what are now Arizona, New Mexico, Texas, Nevada and Utah. Goods came by ship round Baja California and up the Gulf of California, then by river steamer to Yuma, and went out again by boat and by mule-team wagon; the army aimed to keep six months\' supply on hand. The Signal Corps was here from 1875 to 1891. The park opened in 1997 and is now Colorado River State Historic Park.',
  'Five buildings left from the depot. The commanding officer\'s quarters is a house museum set up as it might have looked in the 1870s. Outside are a stone-built reservoir, a steam boiler, a steamboat paddle axle the park thinks most likely came from the Searchlight, wagons and shade ramadas with panels on Yuma\'s early irrigation.',
  'Where the supplies for every fort in the territory came off the river.',
  'Colorado River State Historic Park, Arizona State Parks, at 201 North 4th Avenue, Yuma. Fees from the park\'s facility page, read November 2026: adults (14 and over) $10. No pets, bicycles or skateboards in the park; no food or open drinks in the buildings. Closed Thanksgiving, Christmas Eve and Christmas Day.',
  [W('Colorado River State Historic Park'),['Arizona State Parks: Colorado River, facility information','https://azstateparks.com/colorado-river/explore/facility-information']],
  conf=None,brick='?',hours='October to May Tuesday to Sunday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; June to September Thursday to Sunday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.'),
N('swansea-az','Swansea','Bouse','az','town','ruins',34.16877,-113.850469,'site','public','drive','','1908–1937',
  'A copper camp in the desert east of Parker. Miners Newton Evans and Thomas Jefferson Carrigan raised money once the railroad was coming, and by the end of 1908 a 350-ton furnace, a 3.5-mile pipeline from the Bill Williams River and hoists for five shafts were going up; by 1909 about 500 people lived here, and the Arizona and Swansea Railroad ran from Bouse from 1910. The first company failed by 1912. The American Smelting and Refining Company bought the mines in 1914 and rebuilt much of the town, and the Depression closed them for good in 1937.',
  'Several adobe and brick buildings, the railroad grade, two cemeteries, many stone foundations and the mine shafts. The Bureau of Land Management has put roofs back on rows of single miners\' quarters and laid out an interpretive trail. Weather and vandalism are wearing the town down. Open shafts and tunnels are dangerous: keep away from them. Leave every artifact, down to rusty cans and bricks, where it lies.',
  'A copper town with its smelter-era buildings standing alone in the desert.',
  'Public land of the Bureau of Land Management, Lake Havasu Field Office. About 25 miles of graded gravel road from Bouse or from Parker; the BLM page says the last few miles are rough and a high-clearance vehicle is recommended, but four-wheel drive is not needed. From Bouse the road passes the old railroad grade and the Midway junction with a bulletin board. No services; hours not given.',
  [W('Swansea, Arizona'),['BLM: Swansea Historic Ghost Town','https://www.blm.gov/visit/swansea-historic-ghost-town']],
  conf=None,brick='some'),
N('durlin-hotel-oatman','Durlin Hotel (Oatman Hotel)','Oatman','az','hotel','standing',35.02642,-114.38341,'exact','open','drive','','1924',
  'A hotel on Oatman\'s Main Street, which was also U.S. Route 66 from 1926 to 1952. The National Register and Wikipedia give it as built in 1924 by John Durlin; the National Park Service page says it was first built in 1902 and rebuilt in 1924 after the fire of 1921 that burned most of the town. Clark Gable and Carole Lombard stopped here after their 1939 wedding in Kingman. It was renamed the Oatman Hotel in the late 1960s.',
  'The only historic two-storey adobe building in Mohave County. The NPS page says it no longer takes overnight guests but has a bar and restaurant on the ground floor, whose walls and ceiling are papered with signed dollar bills, and a museum upstairs. Interior otherwise not checked.',
  'The Route 66 gold-camp hotel papered in dollar bills.',
  'Privately owned; a working bar, restaurant and museum at 181 Main Street (Oatman Road, old Route 66). Opening hours and parking not checked.',
  [W('Durlin Hotel'),['NPS: Durlin Hotel','https://www.nps.gov/places/durlin-hotel.htm']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('pipe-spring-winsor-castle','Winsor Castle, Pipe Spring','Fredonia','az','fort','standing',36.86326,-112.73964,'exact','open','drive','','1870s',
  'A spring on the Arizona Strip used by Ancestral Puebloans and Kaibab Paiutes for at least 1,000 years, named by a Latter-day Saint party in 1858. Mormon settlers from St. George, Utah, brought cattle in the 1860s; after a raid during the Black Hawk War a fort was built over the main spring by 1872. Brigham Young bought it for the church, and Anson Perry Winsor ran the ranch, so the fort was called Winsor Castle. It became a national monument in 1923.',
  'The fort, Winsor Castle, shown on daily tours, with an orchard and garden, summer living-history demonstrations and a half-mile trail; the visitor centre and museum are shared with the Kaibab Band of Paiute Indians and tell both stories. Programs, tours and hikes are seasonal: ask at the front desk.',
  'A stone ranch fort built over the spring it guarded.',
  'Pipe Spring National Monument, National Park Service, on the Kaibab Paiute Reservation west of Fredonia. Hours from the monument\'s hours page (last updated November 2024): the bookstore is open daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day. Times are Arizona time. Fees not checked.',
  [W('Pipe Spring National Monument'),['NPS: Pipe Spring hours','https://www.nps.gov/pisp/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  th=[36.86261,-112.73709],thn='Pipe Spring visitor centre lot',conf=None,brick='?',hours='Daily 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Arizona time; tours seasonal'),
N('riordan-mansion-flagstaff','Riordan Mansion','Flagstaff','az','landmark','standing',35.18733,-111.65964,'exact','open','drive','','1904',
  'A double house built in 1904 for the lumber-baron brothers Timothy and Michael Riordan, who had married sisters, Caroline and Elizabeth Metz. It is about 13,000 square feet: two matching 6,000-square-foot wings, one for each family, joined by a big common room. The architect, Charles Whittlesey, also designed the El Tovar Hotel at the Grand Canyon. The state took the house, much of its contents and five acres in 1978.',
  'Arts and Crafts outside and in: massive stone arches at the porch corners, log-slab siding, wood shingles and native stone. The inside is seen only on the guided tour, through rooms set up as if the family had just stepped out; it ends in the West House, with exhibits on Arts and Crafts, Native American pottery and baskets, photographic window panels, logging and a model of old Flagstaff.',
  'Two lumber barons\' houses under one roof, with the furniture still in place.',
  'Riordan Mansion State Historic Park, Arizona State Parks, next to Northern Arizona University. Main entrance and lot at 409 West Riordan Road, two blocks east of Milton Road (use that address in a satnav). Fees from the park\'s facility page, read November 2026: grounds $5 for adults; the house tour $18 for adults, reservations recommended. Christmas closed.',
  [W('Riordan Mansion State Historic Park'),['Arizona State Parks: Riordan Mansion, facility information','https://azstateparks.com/riordan-mansion/explore/facility-information']],
  conf=None,brick='no',hours='Daily; April to October 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m., tours hourly 10 to 4; November to March 10:30 a.m. to 5 p.m., tours hourly 11 to 4'),
N('wupatki-pueblo','Wupatki Pueblo','Flagstaff','az','town','ruins',35.52113,-111.37288,'exact','public','walk','','1100s–1225',
  'A pueblo of more than 100 rooms with a community room and a ballcourt, the largest building for nearly 50 miles, built by Ancestral Puebloan people north of Flagstaff. Settlement grew after Sunset Crater erupted in the late 1000s and its ash helped the soil hold water. About 85 to 100 people lived here by 1182, and by 1225 it was left for good. Hopi clans and other descendant communities still return to it.',
  'The NPS calls it the largest free-standing pueblo in northern Arizona: 104 rooms, the ballcourt and a large community room. An overlook a few hundred feet behind the visitor centre, on an accessible path, shows the whole pueblo; a half-mile round-trip trail goes up close, with the ballcourt and a blowhole at its far end. Nearby on the park road are the Wukoki and Citadel pueblos.',
  'A red-rock pueblo with a ballcourt and a breathing blowhole.',
  'Wupatki National Monument, National Park Service, on the loop road off U.S. 89 north of Flagstaff, 13 miles from the north entrance; the pueblo is directly behind the Wupatki visitor centre, where you park. The hours page (read November 2026) says the park road is open all the time and trails and pueblos sunrise to sunset all year. Stay on the trail; the sites are cared for by their descendants. Fees not checked.',
  [W('Wupatki National Monument'),['NPS: Visit Wupatki Pueblo','https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/visit-wupatki-pueblo.htm'],['NPS: Wupatki hours','https://www.nps.gov/wupa/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  th=[35.52024,-111.37079],thn='Wupatki visitor centre lot',conf=None,brick='?',hours='Trails and pueblos sunrise to sunset, all year'),
N('hubbell-trading-post','Hubbell Trading Post','Ganado','az','landmark','standing',35.70926,-109.55847,'site','open','drive','','1878',
  'The oldest trading post still working on the Navajo Nation. John Lorenzo Hubbell bought a post in the Ganado valley in 1878, ten years after the Navajo were allowed home from Bosque Redondo, and in 1883 began a solid building of local sandstone on the south bank of the Pueblo Colorado Wash. For a century such posts were bank, pawn shop, post office and store, and Navajo traded wool, sheep and later rugs, jewellery and baskets for goods. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1960 and a National Historic Site in 1965.',
  'The trading post still trades. Visitors may walk round the barn, the animals, the guest hogan and the post; talks, walks and peeks into the Hubbell home are offered when staff allow. Interior of the home otherwise not checked.',
  'A trading post that has never stopped trading.',
  'Hubbell Trading Post National Historic Site, National Park Service, at Ganado, within the Navajo Nation. Hours from the NPS hours page, read November 2026: gate, trading post and visitor centre daily; may close on Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day. The site keeps Navajo Nation time, which is an hour ahead of the rest of Arizona from March to November.',
  [W('Hubbell Trading Post National Historic Site'),['NPS: Hubbell Trading Post hours','https://www.nps.gov/hutr/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  conf=None,brick='no',hours='Daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. (Navajo Nation time)'),
N('la-posada-winslow','La Posada Hotel and Santa Fe Depot','Winslow','az','hotel','standing',35.02147,-110.69519,'site','open','drive','','1929–1930',
  'Mary Colter\'s Harvey House on the Santa Fe Railway and Route 66: the depot was built in 1929 and the hotel finished in 1930, and Colter called the hotel her masterpiece. She imagined it as the hacienda of a Spanish rancher of the early 1800s and even faked the ruins of an old fort on the grounds. The hotel closed in 1957, became railroad offices and was then left empty; it was bought in 1997 and restored as a hotel and restaurant. Work began in 2016 to turn the depot side into an art museum.',
  'Mission Revival buildings with adobe walls, red tile roofs, wooden shutters and iron grilles, flagstone floors and beamed ceilings, with entrances on both the train platform and the street. The gardens, planned by Colter but never made in her day, have been restored since 1997; the hotel\'s page describes a walking tour that starts in the entry hall at Colter\'s 1929 garden plan. Amtrak\'s Southwest Chief still stops here daily.',
  'Mary Colter\'s railroad hacienda, saved and working again.',
  'A privately owned working hotel and restaurant at 303 East Second Street (old Route 66); the depot is an Amtrak station. Opening hours of the public rooms and parking not checked.',
  [W('Winslow station (Arizona)'),W('La Posada Historic District'),['NPS: La Posada Historic District','https://www.nps.gov/places/la-posada-historic-district.htm'],['La Posada: the gardens','https://www.laposada.org/gardens']],
  conf=None,brick='no',hours='?'),
N('gila-county-courthouse-globe','Gila County Courthouse (Cobre Valley Center for the Arts)','Globe','az','civic','standing',33.39626,-110.78695,'exact','open','drive','','1906',
  'A four-storey courthouse of 1906 with a three-storey jail of 1909 behind it, where many were hanged. The architect, W. R. Norton, worked in an Italian Renaissance style. It is now the Cobre Valley Center for the Arts.',
  'A symmetrical front of locally quarried stone with pilasters up to an ornate frieze and cornice; the classical trim at the windows and roof line is pressed metal. Inside, brick bearing walls and wood floors. Interior and what the arts centre shows not checked.',
  'A copper town\'s stone courthouse, now full of art.',
  'Owned as an arts centre; 101 North Broad Street in downtown Globe. The arts centre\'s own page could not be opened when this was written: hours and parking not checked.',
  [W('Gila County Courthouse')],
  conf=None,brick='no',hours='?'),
N('second-pinal-county-courthouse','Second Pinal County Courthouse','Florence','az','civic','standing',33.0323,-111.38568,'exact','view','drive','','1891',
  'A three-storey red-brick courthouse of 1891 by the Arizona architect James M. Creighton. Money ran out before clockworks could be bought for its tower, so a clock was painted on with the hands at 11:44. A new courthouse replaced it in 1961; it fell into disrepair and closed in 2005. After restoration work from 2003 and a $6 million renovation, it was rededicated on December 19, 2012 for the county supervisors and other county offices.',
  'Late Victorian brick with an ornate clock tower and its painted clock. Interior not checked. Florence\'s first courthouse, of 1877, is part of McFarland State Historic Park.',
  'The courthouse whose clock has said 11:44 since 1891.',
  'Pinal County offices, at Pinal and 12th streets in Florence. Seen from the street; whether visitors may go in was not checked.',
  [W('Second Pinal County Courthouse')],
  conf=None,brick='yes'),
N('casa-grande-ruins','Casa Grande Ruins (Great House)','Coolidge','az','town','ruins',32.99431,-111.5377,'exact','open','walk','','1200s–about 1450',
  'A Hohokam great house built of caliche in the Gila valley, the people who farmed there with wide irrigation from the early 1200s. It was four storeys at its centre and three round the outside, and may have been left by 1450. In 1892 the land round it became the first prehistoric and cultural reserve in the United States. A shelter roof was put over it in 1932.',
  'The Great House itself, walls of caliche adobe laid in courses with the cracks between courses still plain, under its 1932 roof, inside Compound A with ruins round it. The visitor centre has a museum and a film.',
  'A four-storey Hohokam great house under a 1932 roof.',
  'Casa Grande Ruins National Monument, National Park Service, in Coolidge. Entrance is free (NPS basic information page, November 2026). Hours from the hours page: the gate shuts 15 minutes before closing and everyone must be out of every area at closing; closed Juneteenth, Independence Day, Thanksgiving and Christmas.',
  [W('Casa Grande Ruins National Monument'),['NPS: Casa Grande Ruins hours','https://www.nps.gov/cagr/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  th=[32.99519,-111.53576],thn='Casa Grande Ruins lot, Ruins Drive',conf=None,brick='no',hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('vulture-city-az','Vulture City','Wickenburg','az','town','ruins',33.81856,-112.83084,'site','open','drive','','1863–1942',
  'The town of the Vulture Mine, the gold mine Henry Wickenburg found in 1863 and the most productive in Arizona\'s history: 340,000 ounces of gold and 260,000 of silver by 1942. The post office opened in 1880 with Wickenburg as postmaster, and the town had boarding houses, stores, saloons and a school. Eighteen men are said to have been hanged on an ironwood tree beside his house.',
  'The large 1884 assay office still stands; the Texas Hotel of 1895 and other buildings stand, and the settler home, saloon and 1880 post office are ruins. Wikipedia says some are only ruins and others in fairly good condition.',
  'Arizona\'s richest gold mine and the ruins of its town.',
  'Privately owned; visits are by the owner\'s tours. The owner\'s tour page could not be opened when this was written: hours, fees and parking not checked. Do not wander off on your own.',
  [W('Vulture City, Arizona')],
  conf=None,brick='?',hours='?'),
N('fort-lowell-tucson','Fort Lowell hospital ruins','Tucson','az','fort','ruins',32.26,-110.87389,'area','public','walk','','1873–1891',
  'An army post of 1873 to 1891 at the meeting of the Tanque Verde and Pantano creeks, then on the edge of Tucson, where the Hohokam had lived centuries before. After the army left, the ruins became the village of El Fuerte; the Boy Scouts bought 40 acres in 1945 to save them, roofed the hospital remains in 1953 and sold the land to Pima County in 1957. It is now Fort Lowell Park.',
  'The adobe remains of the post hospital, once its most prominent building, under the protective roof put up in 1953. The Fort Lowell Museum is in the park. The last of the original officers\' quarters stand just west of Craycroft Road; they were not opened to the public because of lead paint.',
  'A cavalry post\'s melting adobe hospital under its own roof.',
  'Fort Lowell Park, City of Tucson, at 2900 North Craycroft Road, with free parking. The Fort Lowell Museum (Tucson Presidio Trust; its page, read November 2026) is open late August to May, Thursday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m., adults $5. Park hours not checked.',
  [W('Fort Lowell (Tucson, Arizona)'),['Tucson Presidio Trust: Fort Lowell Museum','https://tucsonpresidio.com/visit-ft-lowell-museum/']],
  conf=None,brick='no'),
N('tumacacori-mission','Mission San José de Tumacácori','Tumacacori','az','worship','ruins',31.5687,-111.05086,'exact','open','walk','','1757–1848',
  'A Jesuit mission of 1691 by an O\'odham village, moved to this side of the Santa Cruz River after the rebellion of 1751; a first church went up here in 1757. Franciscans took over in 1768 and built the present adobe church, nearly finished but with its bell tower never capped with a dome. The mission was declared abandoned in 1843 and auctioned in 1844, and the O\'odham who still farmed it left after an Apache attack in 1848.',
  'The church, in its present form from 1828, with its decorated façade, the tower that never got its dome, and plaster walls once studded with crushed red brick. Interior not checked.',
  'A roofless-towered mission church standing alone by the Santa Cruz.',
  'Tumacácori National Historical Park, National Park Service, off Interstate 19 south of Tubac. Hours from the NPS hours page, read November 2026: closed Thanksgiving and Christmas. Fees not checked.',
  [W('Mission San José de Tumacácori'),['NPS: Mission San José de Tumacácori','https://home.nps.gov/tuma/learn/historyculture/tumacacori.htm'],['NPS: Tumacácori hours','https://www.nps.gov/tuma/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  conf=None,brick='?',hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('tubac-presidio','Tubac Presidio','Tubac','az','fort','ruins',31.61175,-111.0466,'site','open','walk','','1752',
  'The Spanish fort set up in 1752 after the Pima rebellion of 1751, with about fifty soldiers to guard the settlements and missions of the Santa Cruz valley. From here in 1774 Captain Juan Bautista de Anza set out to find a land route to California, and in 1775 the garrison moved north to found the presidio of Tucson. After Mexican independence it was left, and the settlement was in ruins when Americans came in the late 1840s.',
  'In an underground exhibit, the foundation, walls and plaza floor of the commandant\'s quarters, dug in 1974. The park also has the 1885 territorial schoolhouse, which the state calls the second oldest in Arizona, and a museum with the press that printed Arizona\'s first newspaper in 1859.',
  'The presidio Anza rode out of for California.',
  'Tubac Presidio State Historic Park, Arizona State Parks, in Tubac; day use only, no overnight parking. Fees from the park\'s facility page, read November 2026: adults (14 and over) $10; the visitor centre is free.',
  [W('Presidio San Ignacio de Tubac'),['Arizona State Parks: Tubac Presidio, facility information','https://azstateparks.com/tubac/explore/facility-information']],
  th=[31.61213,-111.04737],thn='Tubac Presidio lot, Burruel Street',conf=None,brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Friday and Sunday 9 a.m. to 3 p.m.; Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Monday'),
]
